WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:04.820
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.820 --> 00:00:10.230
Not so long ago, we talked about a neural
image generator that was able to dream up

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:12.429
beautiful natural scenes.

00:00:12.429 --> 00:00:18.609
It had a killer feature where it would take
as an input, not only the image itself, but

00:00:18.609 --> 00:00:21.470
the labeled layout of this image as well.

00:00:21.470 --> 00:00:27.660
That is a gold mine of information, and including
this indeed opens up a killer application.

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:28.720
Look!

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:34.400
We can even change the scene around by modifying
the labels on this layout, for instance, by

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:39.930
adding some mountains, make a grassy field,
and add a lake.

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:44.170
Making a scene from scratch from a simple
starting point was also possible with this

00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:45.480
technique.

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:51.330
This is already a powerful, learning-based
tool for artists to use as-is, but can we

00:00:51.330 --> 00:00:52.330
go further?

00:00:52.330 --> 00:00:58.390
For instance, would it be possible to choose
exactly what to fill these regions with?

00:00:58.390 --> 00:01:04.190
And this, is what today’s paper excels at,
and it turns out, it can do, much, much more.

00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:05.190
Let’s dive in.

00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:11.280
One, we can provide it this layout, which
they refer to as a semantic mask, and it can

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:17.680
synthesize clothes, pants, and hair in many,
many different ways.

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:19.030
Heavenly.

00:01:19.030 --> 00:01:24.050
If you have a closer look, you see that fortunately,
it doesn’t seem to change any other part

00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:25.200
of the image.

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:30.640
Nothing too crazy here, but please remember
this, and now would be a good time to hold

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:36.550
on to your papers, because two, it can change
the sky or the material properties of the

00:01:36.550 --> 00:01:38.140
floor.

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:39.740
And…wait!

00:01:39.740 --> 00:01:42.280
Are you seeing what I am seeing?

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:47.570
We cannot just change the sky because we have
a lake there, reflecting it, therefore, the

00:01:47.570 --> 00:01:48.920
lake has to change too.

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:49.920
Does it?

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:52.900
Yes, it does!

00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:57.600
It indeed changes other parts of the image
when it is necessary, which is the hallmark

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:02.330
of a learning algorithm that truly understands
what it is synthesizing.

00:02:02.330 --> 00:02:06.760
You can see this effect especially clearly
at the end of the looped footage when the

00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:09.140
sky is the brightest.

00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:10.379
Loving it.

00:02:10.379 --> 00:02:12.560
So what about the floor?

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:13.810
This is one of my favorites!

00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:19.840
It doesn’t just change the color of the
floor itself, but it performs proper material

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:20.840
modeling.

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:24.819
Look, the reflections also became glossier
over time.

00:02:24.819 --> 00:02:30.739
A proper light transport simulation for this
scenario would take a very, very long time,

00:02:30.739 --> 00:02:33.730
we are likely talking from minutes to hours.

00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:38.709
And this thing has never been taught about
light transport and learned about these materials

00:02:38.709 --> 00:02:40.879
by itself!

00:02:40.879 --> 00:02:46.129
Make no mistake, these may be low-resolution,
pixelated images, but this still feels like

00:02:46.129 --> 00:02:47.329
science fiction.

00:02:47.329 --> 00:02:52.579
Two more papers down the line, and we will
see HD videos of this I am sure.

00:02:52.579 --> 00:02:58.169
The third application is something that the
authors refer to as appearance mixture, where

00:02:58.169 --> 00:03:04.809
we can essentially select parts of the image
to our liking and fuse these aspects together

00:03:04.809 --> 00:03:06.470
into a new image.

00:03:06.470 --> 00:03:12.000
This could, more or less be done with traditional,
handcrafted methods too, but four, it can

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:18.059
also do style morphing, where we start from
image A, change it until it looks like image

00:03:18.059 --> 00:03:20.069
B, and back.

00:03:20.069 --> 00:03:26.120
Now, normally, this can be done very easily
with a handcrafted method called image interpolation,

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:32.439
however, to make this morphing really work,
the tricky part is that all of the intermediate

00:03:32.439 --> 00:03:35.290
images have to be meaningful.

00:03:35.290 --> 00:03:39.439
And as you can see, this learning method does
a fine job at that.

00:03:39.439 --> 00:03:42.549
Any of these intermediate images can stand
on their own.

00:03:42.549 --> 00:03:46.980
I’ll try to stop the morphing process at
different points so you can have a look and

00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:48.680
decide for yourself.

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:51.010
Let me know in the comments if you agree.

00:03:51.010 --> 00:03:57.040
I am delighted to see that these image synthesis
algorithms are improving at a stunning pace,

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:06.939
and I think these tools will rapidly become
viable to aid the work of artists

00:04:06.939 --> 00:04:12.510
in the industry.

00:05:02.180 --> 00:05:05.509
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

